1. Instruction tables (GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The translator of machine code (in lib/libcuda.so.1, lib/libnvJitLink.so.13 and bin/vf)
contains tables of the instruction encodings of NVIDIA's sm_70 and sm_75 architectures: the crate
crates/vf-tables of the source. They are generated by reference/generate.py from the
instruction descriptions in the data directory of the denvdis project
(https://github.com/redplait/denvdis, commit 3769cb8a628f; distributed under the GNU Lesser
General Public License, version 2.1), which that project extracted from NVIDIA's disassembler.
The descriptions themselves are not distributed here; the tables derived from them are, under
the same licence.

Those three binaries are works that use the tables. Their complete source, the tables and the
program that generates them included, is the source repository of this project at the commit
that the file VERSION of a binary archive names, and is offered from the same place as the
archive. With it anyone can change the tables and build the binaries again (scripts/build.sh).
Nothing in the licence of the rest restricts modifying the binaries for one's own use or
reverse engineering them to debug such modifications.

2. No NVIDIA software
---------------------

The archive contains no file of NVIDIA's. forward/bf16tc/libvf_cublas_bf16.so is built against
crates/vf-fwdgen/bf16tc/decls.h, a header written for this project that declares the part of
the programming interfaces of NVIDIA's CUDA driver, cuBLAS and cuBLASLt that the runtime calls:
the names, types and values of those functions, enumerations and structures as NVIDIA's CUDA
12.9 headers define them, which vf-fwdgen checks against those headers when it builds the
runtime. It contains no text of NVIDIA's headers or documentation. These interfaces are
NVIDIA's; they are declared here only so that the runtime can work with NVIDIA's libraries,
which it opens at run time and does not contain. NVIDIA publishes declarations of the same
interfaces under the Apache License 2.0 in its cuda-python and nvmath-python projects
(https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuda-python, https://github.com/NVIDIA/nvmath-python). The
forwarding libraries (forward/) are generated from lists of symbol names and contain no NVIDIA
code. forward/bf16tc/libvf_cublas_bf16.so, the runtime of the opt-in bf16 cuBLAS variants,
holds sm_70 machine code that NVIDIA's CUDA 12.9.2 compiler made from this project's source
crates/vf-fwdgen/bf16tc/kernels.cu, which calls no routine of an NVIDIA library: it uses only
built-in functions of the CUDA language (such as __ldg and __shfl_xor_sync), which the compiler
turns into single instructions (crates/vf-fwdgen/bf16tc/kernels.manifest;
scripts/build-bf16-kernels.sh rebuilds it to the same bytes). scripts/fetch-redist.py downloads
the CUDA 12.9 libraries that a user needs from NVIDIA's own public redistributable archives,
under NVIDIA's licence, onto the user's machine.

